WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.980
We often hear that public universities
charge higher and higher tuition because

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:13.340
state policymakers have cut their
funding. Some say then the way to bring

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:18.200
tuition down is to bring government
funding up. Yet most research finds that

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.020
when state governments cut or increase
funding to public universities,

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:27.020
the effect on tuition is quite weak. One
rigorous study found that for every additional

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.300
dollar public universities received from
taxpayers, tuition declined by only 26 cents.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:37.140
So where's the money going? Well
for one, a university could allocate a

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.600
budget increase to boost its own
spending on new programs and services.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:47.960
In this case, funding goes up, and university
spending goes up, so tuition stays the same.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.920
Now consider a university facing
budget cuts. One 2018 study found that

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.460
universities often reduced their own
spending instead of passing all the

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.100
budget cuts on to students through higher tuition.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:06.540
The bottom line is: not all funding cuts are bad, and
not all funding increases make tuition more affordable.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.600
What do you think is driving tuition
increases? Let us know in your comments.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.640
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like
our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.040
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

